 AI assistants?\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"A new Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) has been submitted for review. Please analyze the following DEP and respond with only \\\"APPROVE\\\" if you approve it, or \\\"DENY\\\" followed by your reasoning if you deny it.\\n\\n---BEGIN USER CLARIFICATION HISTORY---\\nQuestion: Welcome to Double Act AI! Tell us a little about yourself. Who are you and what do you do? Are you a computer expert or are you new to software?\\nResponse: [object Object]\\n\\nQuestion: What are your goals for this project? What led you to pursue this? Where did the idea come from?\\nResponse: My goals are to create an HOA website that allows easy access to information relevant to current and potential residents. I saw similar HOA sites online and was inspired.\\n\\nQuestion: What are specific requirements you have in mind? What is your vision for the completed product?\\nResponse: The pages I would like are Contact Us, Legal Docs, Schools, Dues, Annual Meetings, and News & Events. Dues, Annual Meetings, and News & Events should require sign-in and should be resident only. I envision a simple website that is used for information access.\\n\\nQuestion: What else should we know about you or your project? Any other context or details you want to share? The system will follow-up with questions automatically when needed.\\nResponse: Also, since I know pretty much nothing about technology, I would like to have a simple and user-friendly interface that is easy to navigate. I would also like to have a contact form for residents to reach out with questions or concerns. Since I am unskilled with technology, the blog/updates section should be easy to update without needing to know any coding or anything like that.\\n\\nQuestion: What is the HOA’s official name and city/state so the site can be clearly labeled for visitors and residents?\\nResponse: Dunbar Trees, Farmington Hills, MI\\n\\nQuestion: Who are the main audiences you want to serve (e.g., current residents, prospective buyers, board members), and which audience should be the top priority?\\nResponse: Mainly the first two, board members should have easy editing access though\\n\\nQuestion: For resident-only pages, how would you like residents to get access (for example, describe whether you prefer invite-only, approve-after-signup, or another approach)?\\nResponse: Signup then approve with email confirmation of approval\\n\\nQuestion: Who should be allowed to add or edit content on the site (e.g., you only, specific board members, committee leads), and should changes require approval before they go live?\\nResponse: Board members\\n\\nQuestion: On the Dues page, what information should be shown (e.g., current rates, due dates, how/where to pay, FAQs)? Do you want to provide information only, or also allow residents to pay online?\\nResponse: I will have PDFs and pay online would be awesome\\n\\nQuestion: For Annual Meetings, what items do you want to include (e.g., upcoming meeting dates, agendas, minutes, recordings) and do you need RSVP or reminders?\\nResponse: Just PDFs\\n\\nQuestion: For News & Events, what format do you prefer (e.g., news posts, a calendar view, event detail pages) and do you want RSVPs, reminders, or comments?\\nResponse: News posts with text and images supported\\n\\nQuestion: For the Schools page, which schools or school levels should be listed, and should this page include external links or boundary maps?\\nResponse: Should be similar to news page\\n\\nQuestion: For Legal Docs, which document categories do you need (e.g., bylaws, CC&Rs, policies, architectural forms), and should any of these be public while others remain resident-only?\\nResponse: Bylaws\\n\\nQuestion: For the Contact Us form, where should messages be sent (e.g., a single email address, multiple recipients by topic)? What fields do you want (name, address, email, phone, topic, message), and do you want file attachments?\\nResponse: Single email\\n\\nQuestion: Would you like residents to optionally receive email notifications when new News, Events, or Annual Meeting updates are posted? If yes, who chooses which notifications to receive (the resident or an admin)?\\nResponse: Yes (resident)\\n\\nQuestion: Do you want a simple site-wide search so users can quickly find pages, posts, and documents?\\nResponse: No\\n\\nQuestion: Do you have a logo, colors, or style preferences for a simple, clean look? If not, how would you describe the desired feel (e.g., friendly, formal, modern, minimal)?\\nResponse: No, minimal and friendly\\n\\nQuestion: Are there any accessibility or readability needs (e.g., larger text, high contrast, simple language) or language considerations we should account for?\\nResponse: No\\n\\nQuestion: Should the site be optimized for phones and tablets, since many residents may visit on mobile devices?\\nResponse: Yes\\n\\nQuestion: Do you have any privacy, legal disclaimers, or policies that must be displayed, and is there any personal information you do NOT want to collect?\\nResponse: No\\n\\nQuestion: What content do you already have ready (e.g., documents, text for pages, images, logos), and what content still needs to be created?\\nResponse: No\\n\\nQuestion: What tasks do you want to comfortably do yourself without technical knowledge (e.g., post news/events, upload documents, edit page text, manage resident access, send announcements)?\\nResponse: All of those\\n\\nQuestion: In your own words, what would make this site a success for you in the first 3 months after launch?\\nResponse: Happy residents getting information from the site!\\n\\nQuestion: Is there anything else that is a must-have or must-not-have that we haven’t covered?\\nResponse: No\\n\\n---END USER CLARIFICATION HISTORY---\\n\\n---BEGIN DEP---\\nUser Wish: Dunbar Trees HOA Website\\n\\nPersona and Goals\\n- Primary admin persona: A non-technical HOA board member of Dunbar Trees (Farmington Hills, MI) who wants a simple, friendly website to keep residents informed without needing any coding knowledge.\\n- Audiences: (1) Current residents (top priority for private content), (2) Prospective buyers/public (basic info), (3) Board members (need easy editing access).\\n- Success (first 3 months): Residents regularly access information; they can easily view/download documents, receive update emails by choice, and pay dues online; the board can update content without technical hurdles.\\n\\nScope and Pages\\nPublic pages (no sign-in required):\\n- Home: Simple landing page with HOA name and location, short welcome blurb, and clear navigation to all sections, plus a visible resident sign-in/sign-up link.\\n- Contact Us: Simple form that sends messages to a single designated HOA email address. Fields: name, email, message. Confirmation shown after submission.\\n- Legal Docs: Public listing of bylaws (PDFs) for viewing/downloading.\\n- Schools: Public, post-style entries similar to a news page, allowing text, images, and links to external school resources. No comments.\\n\\nResident-only pages (sign-in required):\\n- Dues: Board-uploaded PDFs (e.g., dues info). Residents can pay dues online through a simple, secure flow and receive an email receipt. The page shows the resident their most recent payment status.\\n- Annual Meetings: List of PDFs (e.g., agendas and minutes) for viewing/downloading. Residents may opt in to email notifications when new meeting PDFs are posted.\\n- News & Events: Feed of board-posted updates with text and images. No comments, RSVPs, or calendar views. Residents can opt in to email notifications for new posts.\\n\\nAccess, Roles, and Permissions\\n- Account creation for residents: Residents self-sign up; a board admin approves accounts. After approval, residents receive an email confirmation and can access resident-only pages.\\n- Roles:\\n  - Resident: View resident-only sections, manage their email notification preferences, and pay dues online.\\n  - Board editor/admin: Add/edit/publish posts and pages (no extra approval workflow required), upload PDFs and images, manage resident approvals, and send announcement emails.\\n\\nContent Management (non-technical)\\n- Simple, user-friendly admin area for board members with a WYSIWYG editor for posts/pages (no coding required).\\n- Drag-and-drop uploads for PDFs and images with basic image placement in posts.\\n- Clearly labeled forms and step-by-step prompts for common tasks (post news, upload documents, edit page text, manage resident access, send announcements).\\n\\nNotifications and Announcements\\n- Residents can opt in/out of email notifications by category: (a) News & Events, (b) Annual Meetings updates, (c) Dues updates.\\n- Board admins can send announcement emails to opted-in residents with a simple subject/body editor.\\n\\nDesign and Usability\\n- Look and feel: Minimal, friendly, and clean, with straightforward navigation.\\n- Mobile-optimized: Works well on phones and tablets.\\n- No site-wide search.\\n\\nPrivacy and Data\\n- Collect only what’s needed for function: resident sign-up (name and email) and contact form (name, email, message). No special privacy policies or disclaimers requested by the user.\\n\\nOut of Scope / Non-requirements\\n- No comments, RSVPs, calendar views, complex approval workflows, or site-wide search.\\n\\nAssumptions\\n- Legal Docs currently include only bylaws.\\n- Schools content is informational posts (public), managed by board editors.\\n- Online dues payments include an emailed receipt to the payer; additional financial reporting is not required.\\n\\nDefinition of Done\\n- All listed pages exist with the specified public/resident-only access.\\n- Board admins can create and publish posts with text and images, and upload PDFs.\\n- Resident sign-up with admin approval and email confirmation works.\\n- Residents can set notification preferences and receive emails for chosen categories.\\n- Dues page supports online payment and sends